s primarily in the United States, Canada, and Europe. Its products include plastic reusable containers, pallets, small parts bins, bulk shipping containers, storage and organization products, OEM parts, custom plastic products, composite ground protection matting, tire repair and retreading products, and consumer fuel containers, as well as tanks for water, fuel, and waste handling. The company also manufactures and sells various traffic markings, such as reflective highway marking tapes. It serves industrial manufacturing, food processing, retail/wholesale products distribution, agriculture, recreational vehicles, marine vehicles, healthcare, appliance, bakery, electronics, textiles, construction, infrastructure and consumer, and others. The company sells its products directly, as well as through distributors. Myers Industries, Inc. was founded in 1933 and is headquartered in Akron, Ohio.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Aaron M. Schapper | President, CEO & Director | Age 51 | Compensation: $1.5M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 2K people. Revenue per employee of $375K ranks in the below the sector median among Consumer Cyclical peers. Each employee generates $31K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $2K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 4\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (3/10).","financial_summary":["Myers Industries, Inc. is a small-cap consumer cyclical business with a $1.1B market cap on $826M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -3% over the past three years and +3% annually across the full decade, and a shrinking top line is the hardest headwind any business can overcome.","Returns on capital run at 14% against a 8% cost of capital, a 6-point spread that creates value, though narrow enough that competitive pressure could close it, with gross margins sitting at 30%.","The business throws off $67M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 8 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 2.48\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is moderate at 3.3\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, serviceable in normal conditions but worth watching through a downturn, and thin 2.7\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 2.1% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Altman Z in the grey zone \u2014 financial health warrants monitoring","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Consumer staples outperforming discretionary, signaling cautious spending","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Consumer demand is fundamentally cyclical \u2014 a recession compresses both unit volume and pricing power, and operating leverage cuts both ways.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"E-commerce margin compression and tariff exposure \u2014 digital competition erodes pricing power while trade policy reshuffles supply-chain cost structures overnight.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Brand relevance decay \u2014 shifting consumer preferences and social-media-driven trends can strand inventory and invalidate positioning faster than product cycles can adapt.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Shares offer a 30% margin of safety against estimated fair value","Market is pricing in lower growth than the company has historically delivered","34 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes